tauceti:TauCetiRoadmap/ProfiniteCohomolo.summary:
Mathlib supplies the canonical carrier with compatible-pair functoriality, and Tau Ceti has built most of the low-degree theory on the explicit model. Built:
- discrete coefficients, continuous sections and the smooth-discrete dictionary (Layers 0-1), apart from the induced actions on G-stable subgroups and quotients and the closure properties of smooth discrete objects;
- the complex with res/infl/coeff and degree-1 conjugation (Layer 2), apart from the ℤ_p worked examples;
- the finite-group comparison and the degree-0 canonical comparison (Layer 3);
- the finite-quotient system in all degrees with its naturality, and the colimit theorem in degrees 0 and 1 (Layer 4);
- the long exact sequence through H² with naturality, and inflation-restriction (Layer 5);
- corestriction in degrees 0-2 with cor∘res (Layer 6);
- coinduction with Shapiro in degrees 0-1 (Layer 7);
- all six cup shapes with associativity, commutativity, restriction, inflation and the projection formula (Layer 8);
- the Kummer sequence, cocycle and injective class map (Layer 9).
Missing:
- the degree-1/2 comparison with continuousCohomology and the degree-2 colimit;
- the transgression and five-term sequence, transitivity and Mackey, and Shapiro in degree 2 with its naturality;
- cup/connecting-map identities and the Bockstein;
- profinite Hilbert 90, hence the Kummer isomorphism;
- the embedding-indexed field-extension bridge (Mathlib has only the intermediate-field fixing-subgroup facts);
- most of Layers 10-13: no canonical all-degree colimit, long exact sequence, corestriction, Shapiro or torsion theorem, no cohomological dimension, no all-degree cup product and no Evens norm. The nearest inputs are Mathlib's discrete group cohomology and Tau Ceti's finite-group Tate corestrictions. TauCeti.WreathProduct already gives the permutation wreath product the norm needs.

tauceti:TauCetiRoadmap/StablePeriodicCur.summary:
The libraries have the foundations but little of the content. Tau Ceti has:
- the ideal-quotient construction;
- relative projectives and injectives in exact structures, with exact functors;
- the Frobenius exact predicate;
- finite-dimensional Frobenius algebras: Nakayama automorphism, two-sided self-injectivity, and injectivity of principal projectives over a self-injective ring.
Mathlib supplies:
- the quotient universal properties, including the first isomorphism theorem for quotient categories;
- complexes of any shape, so differential modules and ZMod n-periodic complexes exist as HomologicalComplex of shape refl Unit or up' 1, with homotopy categories and cones;
- localization at quasi-isomorphisms, of complexes and of homotopy categories, and bounded derived categories;
- projective and injective dimension, K-projective complexes, regular local rings and Rees's theorem;
- the Verdier-localization, envelope and colimit-closure machinery.
Missing:
- the factoring ideal and Stable E, Happel's triangulation and stable module categories;
- Gorenstein-projectives, singularity categories and Buchweitz;
- shifts and triangulations on periodic homotopy and derived categories, compression and orbit categories;
- curved DG algebras and modules (Tau Ceti's DG/A∞ theory is uncurved) and derived categories of the second kind;
- matrix factorizations, with depth, Cohen–Macaulay modules and the Eisenbud and Orlov theorems.

AutomorphicCongruences.summary:
Essentially nothing specific to this roadmap is in the libraries. What exists:
- general commutative algebra: module length, heights and Krull's theorem, orders of vanishing, Weierstrass preparation, the PID structure theorem, regular local rings and regular sequences, and the height-one intersection theorem for Dedekind domains;
- low-degree continuous group cohomology with inflation–restriction, corestriction and Shapiro;
- Tau Ceti's Galois-cohomological Kummer map Kˣ/(Kˣ)ⁿ → H¹(G_K, μₙ), proved injective only;
- Kummer and 2-descent Selmer groups, defined by valuations and local points rather than by Galois-cohomological local conditions;
- GL₂ Hecke rings, U_p, Eisenstein series and the q-expansion principle over ℂ.
Missing:
- congruence modules, pseudorepresentations and Galois (Ribet-style) extension classes; only generic Ext¹ classes and H² classes of group extensions exist;
- characteristic and Fitting ideals;
- unitary Shimura varieties and their Eisenstein families;
- Beilinson–Flach classes and Yager modules;
- Galois deformation rings with R = T and patching;
- zeta morphisms and determinant lines;
- Hilbert Hida families and Heegner points;
- every p-adic L-function and main-conjecture statement.

AutomorphicPadicLFunctions.summary:
None of the four constructions is in the libraries, and the gap is structural. What exists is finite-level arithmetic and one-variable input:
- Tau Ceti's ray class groups for any modulus, with surjective transition maps, and the ideal-theoretic Artin map;
- Mathlib's idele class group, and Dirichlet characters with conductors and Euler factors, which are the finite-order Hecke characters of ℚ;
- abstract measures on any topological space, with the ℤ_p Amice transform (linear only: ℤ_p⟦X⟧ is a ring, but there is no convolution or completed group ring);
- values of ζ and Hurwitz ζ at negative integers, the Dedekind zeta series, and CM fields;
- Eisenstein series and L-functions of modular forms over ℚ;
- Tau Ceti's U_p at bad primes and sheaf cohomology of 𝒪_X-modules on schemes.
Missing:
- the p-power-conductor limit and its character space;
- Hecke characters over general number fields with infinity types, and distributions on ray-class quotients;
- all cohomology of arithmetic groups over number fields, and overconvergent coefficients;
- Hilbert and Bianchi forms, Deligne–Ribet, Katz and Hsieh;
- the unitary Shimura-variety, doubling and Klingen machinery of EHLS and Eischen–Wan.
There is no p-adic L-function of any kind at this pin.

ColemanIntegration.summary:
The libraries supply the analytic scaffolding but none of the p-adic theory. Mathlib has:
- power series with radius of convergence over any nontrivially normed field, restricted series and Tate algebras, and ℂ_p;
- termwise differentiation, Kähler differentials and the analytic exterior derivative;
- the Frobenius-lift predicate IsArithFrobAt;
- the complex values of polylogarithms at roots of unity (as expZeta), with the Fourier-transform and Gauss-sum identities for Dirichlet L-functions;
- p-adic measures with the Mahler and Amice transforms.
Tau Ceti adds Huber pairs, adic spectra, restricted and two-sided restricted series, the formal-group description of the residue disc of the origin on an elliptic curve, and the residue theorem for Weil differentials of function fields. Genuinely missing:
- the p-adic logarithm and its branches;
- annuli with Laurent series and residues, and primitives on non-archimedean discs;
- Frobenius lifts on curves, and overconvergent and rigid/de Rham cohomology;
- Coleman functions and integration, and p-adic polylogarithms;
- p-adic L-functions and RJW Theorem 6.7;
- all regulators beyond the real-valued ones.
